WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.960
A gravitational arc candidate at redshift near 4.4 has appeared in public james web data. Revealing structure from when the universe was less than 1.5 billion years old. This is fascinating frontiers, episode 155. Astronomer Homer Davila Gutierrez identified the feature in images of the galaxy cluster, MACS J0308.9 plus 2645. The arc shows a photometric redshift

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:50.900
near 4.4 and had not been cataloged before. The discovery paper is under review at publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan. Follow-up spectroscopy will be needed to confirm the distance and magnification. The arc appears stretched by the cluster's mass, offering a magnified glimpse of an early galaxy.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:01:16.560
The newly identified gravitational arc at photometric redshift near 4.4 offers a magnified view of a galaxy from the universe's first 1.5 billion years. Its discovery in archival james web data shows how public data sets continue to yield surprises when examined with fresh eyes. Confirmation will require spectra to pin down the exact distance and lens mass.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:33.840
What fraction of similar high redshift features remain hidden in existing web pointings? Now, on a completely different note, Astrobase has completed the initial test firing of an 800 kilonewton class engine developed entirely by a private Indian team.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:44.140
The engine is intended for a future launch vehicle that would place satellites into low Earth orbit. No prior Indian private company has reached this thrust level.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:51.340
The test marks a step toward reducing reliance on government launch providers for medium-class payloads.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:02:04.320
Next steps include integrated stage testing and a planned orbital demonstration flight. The effort draws on domestic engineering expertise to achieve performance comparable to established medium lift systems.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:21.320
From one early universe surprise to another private sector milestone that could help us reach distant targets more affordably. NASA engineers have adjusted Voyager 2's power management so its remaining science instruments can operate through at least 2027.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:37.220
The 48-year-old probe is now in interstellar space and continues to return data on the heliopause boundary. The latest change involved turning off non-essential heaters and reallocating wattage from the radio isotope thermal electric generator.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:47.720
No new hardware failures have been reported since the adjustment. The spacecraft's instruments now measure plasma waves and magnetic fields far beyond the sun's influence.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:55.500
While new rockets take shape on Earth and established explorer just gained extra time to keep reporting from interstellar space.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:08.180
During its quarterly earnings call, SpaceX confirmed it intends to establish robotic factories on the moon. The company expects autonomous systems to handle most construction and assembly tasks.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:12.700
Initial hardware would support propellant production and structural components.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:26.480
The timeline remains tied to successful starship landings and power infrastructure. This development fits yesterday's coverage of starship progress by showing how recovered hardware could enable sustained lunar operations.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:41.300
While private firms look to the moon, Canada is locking in its contribution to the lunar gateway. The Canadian Space Agency has confirmed continued funding for the Canadarm 3 robotic system destined for the lunar gateway.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:51.300
The arm will feature advanced autonomy and artificial intelligence for maintenance tasks. Canadian industry partners will supply the hardware and ground control systems.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:02.360
Delivery remains aligned with the Artemis program schedule. The design includes multiple end-effectors capable of handling diverse payloads and repair operations.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:10.620
From robotic systems heading to the moon to fresh JWST clues about how galaxies themselves first took shape.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:20.460
New analysis suggests the compact red objects discovered by the James Webb Space Telescope could represent an early stage in spiral galaxy formation.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:27.680
Their spectral features indicate dense stellar cores surrounded by gas that may later settle into disks.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:37.520
The objects appear at red shifts between 4 and 7. Further observations will test whether they grow through mergers or in-place star formation.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:48.100
The findings come from detailed examination of Webb's near infrared spectra. From galaxy formation to another fundamental imprint left by the early universe.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:59.140
Researchers report that the rotation directions of both spiral and elliptical galaxies still carry imprints from tidal torques exerted by surrounding matter in the early universe.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:09.560
The study compared observed spin alignments with predictions from tidal torque theory. Data came from large spectroscopic surveys covering millions of galaxies.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:16.100
The findings support the idea that initial conditions set billions of years ago remained detectable today.

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:27.520
Alignment statistics were measured across multiple redshift bins to trace the signals persistence. Uranus orbits at an average distance of 2.9 billion kilometers.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:36.320
Yet its magnetosphere experiences the solar wind's push on a 17-hour cycle because the planet rolls on its side like a barrel.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:46.940
Imagine standing on the surface while a 400 kilometer per second stream of charged particles slams into a magnetic field that itself spins perpendicular to the flow.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:06:04.280
The bow shock can shift inward or outward by several planetary radii within one rotation. Voyager 2's 1986 flyby recorded only a single moment of this breathing motion, leaving the full daily pattern to be modeled from limited data.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:15.680
The planet's magnetic axis is also offset by roughly 60 degrees from its spin axis, so the shock front never settles into the stable shape seen at Earth or Jupiter.

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:28.580
We can calculate the geometry with increasing precision but the key unanswered question remains, how these repeated compressions and expansions influence the rate at which atmospheric ions escape into space,

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:33.380
and whether any radiation belts can persist under such variable pressure.

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:44.540
The August 8 astronomy picture of the day shows periodic comet 10p slash 10p2 passing near several well-known Messier galaxies and nebulae.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:49.860
The image captures the comet's faint coma against a backdrop of deep sky targets.

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:57.100
Temple 2 reaches Parahelian every 5.5 years and currently lies in the constellation Taurus.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.620
Observers with modest telescopes can still locate it under dark skies this month.

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:10.460
The comet's nucleus is estimated at a few kilometers across and releases dust and gas as it approaches the sun.

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:20.060
NASA Administrator Bill Nelson stated that nuclear fission systems will supply the sustained energy needed for long duration Mars surface operations.

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:26.040
The approach would provide kilowatts of electricity independent of sunlight or dust storms.

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:35.420
Current development focuses on a 10kW class fission surface power unit, engineers plan ground testing before any flight demonstration.

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:43.240
The system is designed to operate continuously through the Martian night and during dust events that reduce solar panel output.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:50.420
Students in Kenya conducted the country's first live amateur radio conversation with the International Space Station.

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:56.240
The contact lasted several minutes and allowed participants to ask questions directly to the crew.

00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:02.020
The event was coordinated through the ARIS program and local radio clubs.

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:06.780
Organizers hope to expand similar educational links in East Africa.

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:12.680
The session demonstrated reliable signal acquisition despite the station's rapid orbital motion.

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:20.040
NASA's Skyfall helicopter concepts will incorporate lightweight capes containing sensors and communication hardware.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:26.540
The fabric-like layers are designed to survive dust and temperature extremes while adding minimal mass.

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:31.680
Each cape integrates with the rotor craft's power and avionic systems.

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:37.060
The technology aims to extend mission range beyond current ingenuity capabilities.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:41.780
The capes also provide thermal regulation during the cold Martian nights.

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:51.060
Engineers are troubleshooting a propulsion or attitude control issue on the private link spacecraft launched last month to service a NASA telescope.

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:56.240
The vehicle remains in a stable orbit while teams upload diagnostic commands.

00:08:56.760 --> 00:09:00.140
Mission managers have not yet set a new rendezvous timeline.

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:05.200
The spacecraft was intended to demonstrate commercial satellite servicing techniques.

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:10.380
Ground teams continue to monitor telemetry for clues to the anomalies origin.

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:18.180
A privately funded mission aimed at boosting a declining NASA astrophysics satellite has run into its own technical setbacks.

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:26.240
The rescue spacecraft must perform a precise rendezvous and attachment before the target observatory loses too much altitude.

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:29.780
Teams are evaluating alternative docking approaches.

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:33.620
No revised launch or rendezvous date has been announced.

00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:39.540
The observatory continues its slow orbital decay while engineers refine the rescue plan.

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:50.840
Industry and academic leaders gathered at the 46th Coast Bar Scientific Assembly to discuss how new engineering approaches can enable future space science missions.

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:58.160
Sessions covered propulsion, materials, and autonomy for deep space observatories and planetary probes.

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:04.240
Participants highlighted the need for tighter integration between instrument teams and spacecraft designers.

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:08.160
The meeting continues through the week in Busan, South Korea.

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:14.020
Discussions also addressed radiation hardened electronics and long duration power systems.

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:19.160
New data keep arriving from both fresh launches and decades old probes.

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:21.700
The sky is far from finished with us.

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:29.040
Keep an eye on upcoming stage integration tests for the new Indian engine as teams prepare for orbital demonstration.

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:32.400
That covers today's space and science news.

